Date: Wed, 8 Dec 93 18:45:30 PST
From: peb@procase.com (Paul Baclace)
Subject: Conceding Opponent's Points

Well put, Tim.  Rather than "concede by assent" which is a bit ambiguous
to me;  "concede silently and withdraw" sounds more accurate.  

The upside is that more thinking can be done before actual concession.
The downside is that the opponent doesn't get to find out what really
is happening in your head.  The implication for opponents that desire
agreement is that they don't seem to get any.

Overall, the more-thinking part is critical to me.  I don't like to make
snap decisions--I'm scientifically inclined and need to ponder.  I was
gratified to read Richard Feynman, in What Do Care What Other People Think?, 
marvel about generals who make fast decisions and I was gratified that he too
found it very strange.


