Date: Wed, 8 Dec 93 22:31:16 -0800
From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: Brave New Extropia? No Thanks.  (Squirrel warning)

                *** acorn for the line-eater ***

 The First Extropian Squirrel (*) pauses from gathering nuts -- an
   easy task in California -- to join the discussion for a while:
    
    On reading about the paucity of Extropian human females, and about
human-male / human-female stereotypes and behavioral differences, the
First Extropian Squirrel conjectures that some aspects of Extropian
social interaction, political and economic preference, and other
attitudes, may be simply explained.  He suggests they are biologically
determinate behaviors fostered by adherence to genetically- or
culturally-programmed norms for human males.

    Thus, in Extropian emphasis on the importance of property rights
and resource-control rights, he sees traces of a prehistory in which
the establishment and defense of territory, to secure the resources it
contained, was crucial for small bands of hominids, and in which males
played a key role in that defense because of greater strength, size
and perhaps dentition.  Similar behavior is seen in such close human
relatives as gorillas and chimpanzees, as well as in other social
animals, such as lions.

    In Extropian dislike for political coercion in any form, he sees
traces of hierarchical male-dominance social structures, in which
submission without a fight acknowledged inferior status, and in which
moving up the hierarchy conferred enormous advantage; namely, the
right to breed.  Similar behavior is seen in many primates and in
other social animals.

    In Extropian willingness to use force or violence, or both, in
pursuit of various ends, and in the styles of debate often encountered
here, he sees further traces of that same hierarchy, and of the means
of moving up and down it.  (He may also see traces of hunting, though
it is not clear to him that human ancestors were substantial
meat-eaters until relatively recently.  Thus, gorillas are herbivores,
and chimpanzees eat meat only occasionally.)

    In the low density of human females on this list, and in their
reported dislike for some Extropian themes, the First Extropian
Squirrel sees further evidence that these interests are particular to
human males.  Gender-linking of these themes would cast doubt on their
rational basis even with no other hint of a biological explanation.

    And if these behaviors are not rational, then surely they are of
no more consequence, and of no greater relevance to transhuman choice
in an era when intelligence is no longer organically based, than are
the biological causes, like PMS or "baby lust", stereotypically abused
by human males to explain the behavior of human females.

    The Squirrel has stated his thesis in provocative terms because it
would be un-squirrel-like to do otherwise -- he, too, has determinate
behaviors.  Yet his intent is serious.  He believes the subject is
important.  For the increasingly rapidity of human cultural
development, and the prospect of upload and singularity, should surely
cause biological entities so bold as to attempt rational behavior, to
cast a jaundiced eye upon the utility of vestiges of evolution left
over from simpler and more primitive times.

                              -- Jay Freeman, First Extropian Squirrel


(*) The First Extropian Squirrel has previously discussed animal rights,
    morality and other Extropianly controversial topics.  He has claimed
    to be an entity from the High Transcend who enjoys social downloads
    into terran lifeforms for slumming and throwing nuts at passers-by.

