Charlie Stross writes:
> A royal commission in the UK recently intensely annoyed the government
> (which is strong on law'n'order, it likes to think) by reporting that
> there is no correlation between broken/one-parent families and crime:
> the correlation is between relative poverty and crime. Note the
> _relative_ in there. If you're subsisting on a dole that's twice the 
> wage of a professional in another country it doesn't make a blind bit 
> of difference, because that dole's still only 10% of the working wage
> and you're surrounded by people more affluent than yourself. To a
> certain mind-set the logical way out of this quandry is not to get a
> job but to go steal something.

  Such studies are rarely scientific and full of holes. Does this study
also explain the rise in _violent_ crime. In the 50s and 60s, when someone
robbed a grocery store at gunpoint, they would take the money and leave.
Now, it's common for the gunmen to visciously gun down the clerk and innocent
bystanders. Does this study also explain random violence in the US where
someone puts a bullet in your head for stepping on their shoes but they
don't rob you afterwards? How about postal workers coming into work
and gunning down a dozen or so coworkers? This study falls flat on its
face when applied to South American countries of which some have _vastly_
bigger rich/poor gaps but none of the associated _violent,mean_spirited_
theivery.

  I don't think the relative gap between rich/poor explains it at all. Hell,
in the US there is a phenomena of _middle class_ crime and even rich kid
crime.

  My own opinion, _illegal physically addictive drugs_ When you see someone
else with a lifestyle you want, you are envious, but your inhibitions and
human empathy usually prevent you from killing them or even stealing from
them. I grew up with a family income <$25k/yr so I know what it's like.
However, if you are physically addicted to a drug which you can't afford,
therein lies the problem. I claim that addicts will resort to far more
desparate measures to satisfy their craving than a poverty stricken
individual.

  I have personal experience with this. My sister has been a heroin addict
since she was 14. She loves my mother dearly, but when she could no longer
afford her habit, she started stealing things from our house and pawning
them. Then she starting stealing money, old lady's social security checks,
and finally when you was "put out" she tried to stab my mother with
a knife. She has now become so polarized by her isolation and the
crowd she hangs out with (young black youths) that human life means nothing
to her. Zero. She will steal/kill/prostitute/anything to satisfy her habit.
About six months ago she stabbed another girl with a butterknife because --
she refused to lend her a cigarette.


  No, I don't buy this liberal theory of crime at all. It doesn't explain
squat and is a mighty _conveinient_ excuse for raising taxes and
increasing socialism. The "War on Poverty" in the US only succeeded
in letting crime and violence expand even more. It's a _cultural_
problem -- the conservatives are right. It's a problem with people's
ethics and values.

 
  I'll tell you another thing about the "single mother" household theory.
Your last post on "boys in the classroom hogging computers" made a valid
point. Boys will continue to test the limits of territory until someone
stops them. I have noticed that many mothers have a problem practicing
tough love and "putting their foot down" as a result, boys growing up in
single-mother households tend not to be used to limits. I have some
anecdotal evidence. Two of my old friends lived with only their mothers.
They did anything they wanted, stayed out all night, never did homework,
skipped school. Their mother practiced no control. They are now dropouts/drug
addicts. A third friend of mind lived with his father. When he started
skipping school and stealing, his father kicked him out. It straightened
him out.

  Obviously, they are some very capable mothers out there. What I'm saying
is that statically, fathers more often set firm limits and rule with
an iron hand, while mothers are more likely to "baby" their children,
spoiling them.

  Ask yourself this (females on the list). Would you put your child out
if he/she tried to kill you? Even after my sister attempted to stab my
mother, my mother was still willing to let her stay in the house
("because she had nowhere else to go"), only my father kept her out.
My mother still gives her money/food and my sister just keeps on
taking advantage of her kindness.


  Anyway, I don't buy this "inequality of wealth" bs as an explanation of
violent crime in America.

